{"b":2}
'); // [{ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }] // From a Uint8Array (zero-copy for ASCII): const buf = new TextEncoder().encode('{"a":1}
{"b":2}
'); const items = …

Parse a complete JSONL input and return an array of all parsed values. ```ts const input = '{"id":1,"name":"Alice"}
{"id":2,"name":"Bob"}
{"id":3,"name":"Charlie"}
'; const records = JSONL.parse(input); console.log(records); // [ // { id: 1, name: "Alice" }, // { id: 2, name: "Bob" }, // { id: 3, name: "Charlie" } // ] Input can be a string or a `Uint8Array`: ```ts const buffer = new TextEncoder().encode('{"a":1}
{"b":2}
'); const results = Bun.JSONL.parse(buffer); // [{ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }] With `Uint8Array` input, Bun skips a UTF-8 BOM at the start of the buffer.

When the input is a `Uint8Array`, you can pass optional `start` and `end` byte offsets: ```ts const buf = new TextEncoder().encode('{"a":1}
{"b":2}
{"c":3}
'); // Parse starting from byte 8 const result = Bun.JSONL.parseChunk(buf, 8); console.log(result.values); // [{ b: 2 }, { c: 3 }] console.log(result.read); // 23 // Parse a specific range const partial = Bun.JSONL.parseChunk(buf, 0, 8); console.log(partial.values); // [{ a: 1 }] The `read` value is always a byte offset into the original buffer. Use it with `TypedArray.subarray()` for zero-copy streaming: ```ts let buf = new …

Unlike `parse()`, `parseChunk()` does not throw on invalid JSON. Instead, it returns the error in the `error` property, along with any values that were successfully parsed before the error: ```ts const input = '{"a":1}
{invalid}
{"b":2}
'; const result = Bun.JSONL.parseChunk(input); console.log(result.values); // [{ a: 1 }] — values parsed before the error console.log(result.error); // SyntaxError console.log(result.read); // 7 — position up to last successful parse
